I opened an unanswered [question on stackoverflow](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/63852602/bookdown-how-to-make-citations-in-footnotes-appear-in-the-chapter-references) a while ago about citations in footnotes missing from end of chapter bibliographies.

A workaround to footnote citations (discovered by Lars) is to include an invisible chunk containing the citations such that they are rendered in the chapter bibliography, [seen here](https://jimhshen.github.io/citationfootnotes/workaround-footnote-citations.html).

```
# Workaround Footnote Citations

The workaround is to include an invisible text chunk.

This is a citation [@johndoe2020].[^note2]

This sentence has another footnote citation.^[This is another footnote that contains a citation @bobjohnson2018]

[^note2]: This footnote contains a citation [@janesmith2019]

::: {.invisible style="display:none"}
@bobjohnson2018
@janesmith2019
:::
```

Another issue I have discovered with chapter-bib citations is that it seems to have issues with CSL files (specifically the chicago-author-date.csl file found on the [Zotero styles repository](https://www.zotero.org/styles)).

A minimal example can be seen [here](https://jimhshen.github.io/citationfootnotes/chapter-bib-issues.html). The [repository is here](https://github.com/jimhshen/citationfootnotes). The YAML header for index.Rmd (where the CSL is defined) is below.

```
--- 
title: "A Minimal Book Example"
author: "Yihui Xie"
date: "`r Sys.Date()`"
site: bookdown::bookdown_site
output: bookdown::gitbook
documentclass: book
bibliography: [book.bib, packages.bib, test.bib]
csl: chicago-author-date.csl
link-citations: yes
github-repo: rstudio/bookdown-demo
description: "This is a minimal example of using the bookdown package to write a book. The output format for this example is bookdown::gitbook."
---
```

